    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/011"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/007"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/001"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/003"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/004"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/009"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/010"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/016"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/006"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/012"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/017"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/item/014"/>
    <diag:targetsComponentType rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/component-type/Cooling%20device"/>
    <diag:targetsComponentType rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/component-type/Cable%20or%20connector"/>
    <diag:considersCause rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0002-0x0027/procedure/01/cause"/>
    <diag:procedureText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Be sure the fan configuration meets the functional requirements of the server.
1.  Be sure the fans are properly seated and working:
a.  Follow the procedures and warnings in the server documentation for removing the access
panels and accessing and replacing fans.
b.  Unseat, and then reseat, each fan according to the proper procedures.
c.  Replace the access panels, and then attempt to restart the server.
2.  Be sure the fan configuration meets the functional requirements of the server.
3.  Be sure no ventilation issues exist. If the server is operated for an extended period of time with the
access panel removed, airflow might be impeded, causing thermal damage to components.
4.  Be sure no POST error messages are displayed while booting the server that indicate temperature
violation or fan failure information.
5.  Use iLO or an optional IML viewer to access the IML to see if any event list error messages relating to
fans are listed.
6.  In the iLO web interface, navigate to the Information &gt; System Information page and verify the
following information:
a.  Click Fans, and then verify the fan status, fan speed, and airflow requirements.
b.  Click Temperatures, and then verify the temperature readings for each location on the
Temperatures tab. If a hot spot is located, check the airflow path for blockage by cables
and other material. A hot spot is not an absolute temperature but is relative to a component
specification. Hot spot is defined as temperature on a sensor within 3°C of a Caution threshold
listed on the Temperature tab.
7.  Replace any required non-functioning fans and restart the server.
8.  Be sure all fan slots have fans or blanks installed. For airflow requirements, see the server
documentation.
9.  Verify the fan airflow path is not blocked by cables or other material.
For additional information, see the Troubleshooting Guide for HPE ProLiant Gen10 servers.
If this issue persists, create an HPE Support Case.
1.  Verify the component is either under warranty or contract. Go to
https://support.hpe.com/hpesc/public/home, and then click Warranty Status.
This enables HPE to ship replacement parts directly to you once a failure has been confirmed. For
specific information about customer replaceable parts, refer to the product maintenance and service
guide.
2.  Under Support Cases, click Access Cases, and then create a support case.
HPE Support will contact you.</diag:procedureText>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">HPE 0x0027 recommendation 1</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0014-0x0005/procedure/01/item/013">
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">HPE 0x0005 printed item 13</rdfs:label>
    <diag:itemText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Run SUT from the command line (On-Demand mode) to update the Smart Array firmware. For more information, see to the HP SUT documents at http://www.hpe.com/info/sut-docs. Service Pack for ProLiant (SPP): Update the Smart Array firmware by downloading the SPP from the Hewlett Packard Enterprise website (http://www.hpe.com/servers/spp). Refer to the SPP documentation at http://www.hpe.com/info/spp/ documentation for instructions on updating the firmware. Note: Downloading an SPP requires a contract with Hewlett Packard Enterprise and an HPE Passport ID. If you do not have an HPE Passport ID, go to http://www.hpe.com/info/insightonline. Note: Downloading the entire SPP can take a significant amount of time. Online Flash components This component provides updated system firmware that can be installed directly on supported operating systems. Additionally, when used in conjunction with the SUM, this Smart Component allows you to update firmware on remote servers from a central location. This remote deployment capability eliminates the need for you to be physically present at the server to perform a firmware update. For specific instructions on updating firmware, see the Smart Update Manager User Guide, at http:// www.hpe.com/info/hpsum/documentation.</diag:itemText>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting#ProcedureItem"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <diag:sourceLabel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">3.</diag:sourceLabel>
    <diag:nestingLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger">0</diag:nestingLevel>
    <diag:itemOrder rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#positiveInteger">13</diag:itemOrder>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Source action_text character span [1181, 2446); FULL_TEXT_SOURCE_MARKERS; order is printed/text order, not an execution dependency.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Purpose assessment: SOURCE_ANCHORED_RULE_LINK. Explicit action verb and shared documented component/condition in the same source group; inferred intended purpose only.</rdfs:comment>
    <diag:addressesCause rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0014-0x0005/procedure/01/cause"/>
  </rdf:Description>
