    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0011-0x0008/procedure/01/item/004"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting#RecoveryProcedure"/>
    <diag:considersCause rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0011-0x0008/procedure/01/cause"/>
    <diag:targetsComponentType rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/component-type/Cable%20or%20connector"/>
    <diag:procedureText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Refer to the NIC issues flowchart in the Troubleshooting Guide for HPE ProLiant Gen10 servers.
1.  Be sure both ends of cable are connected and not loose.
2.  Go to the Hewlett Packard Enterprise Support website to verify and install available updates for the
following:
◦ Adapter firmware
◦ Adapter driver
◦ System ROM.
3.  Be sure the device at the other end of the connection is on and functioning properly.
4.  Be sure the network port is not disabled in the operating system.
5.  Use a different cable, a different port on the switch, or use a different switch.
If this issue persists, create an HPE Support Case.
1.  Verify the component is either under warranty or contract. Go to the:
Customer Self Repair website.
This enables HPE to ship replacement parts directly to you once a failure has been confirmed. For
specific information about customer replaceable parts, refer to the product maintenance and service
guide.
2.  Create a support case at: http://www.hpe.com/support/hpesc.
HPE Support will contact you.</diag:procedureText>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">HPE 0x0008 recommendation 1</rdfs:label>
  </rdf:Description>
