    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/016"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/005"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/011"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/019"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/001"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/008"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/020"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/002"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/022"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/015"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/021"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/009"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/007"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/003"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/004"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/017"/>
    <diag:hasItem rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/item/012"/>
    <diag:considersCause rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0382/procedure/01/cause"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting#RecoveryProcedure"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">HPE 0x0382 recommendation 1</rdfs:label>
    <diag:procedureText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">Disable Sub-NUMA Clustering or Sanitize all NVDIMMs on Proc %1.
To enable this NVDIMM, either Sub Numa Clustering system configuration must be set as Disabled or all
NVDIMMs on identified processor needs to be sanitized to work with present system configuration. If manual
data backup of NVDIMM is desired, set system configuration as Sub Numa Clustering disabled to match with
NVDIMM configuration and take NVDIMM data backup. Chose desired system configuration afterwards.
Enabling or disabling Sub-NUMA Clustering
NOTE: Enable XPT Prefetcher in the Processor Prefetcher Options to make Sub-NUMA Clustering
configurable.
1.  From the UEFI System Utilities screen, select System Configuration &gt; BIOS/Platform Configuration
(RBSU) &gt; Power and Performance Options &gt; Sub-NUMA Clustering.
2.  Select a setting.
◦ Enabled (default)
◦ Disabled
3.  Save your setting.
NOTE: Up to 1 GB of system memory might become unavailable when Sub-NUMA Clustering is enabled.
Sanitizing NVDIMMs
1.  From the System Utilities screen, select System Configuration &gt; BIOS/Platform Configuration
(RBSU) &gt; System Options &gt; Memory Operations &gt; NVDIMM Memory Options.
2.  Select NVDIMM-N Sanitize/Erase on Next Reboot Policy.
The following alert is displayed.
IMPORTANT: Sanitizing/Erasing an NVDIMM-N will result in the loss of ALL user data saved in the
NVDIMM-N. Hewlett Packard Enterprise recommended that manual backup of all user data in the
NVDIMM-N(s) be performed before sanitizing/erasing the NVDIMM-N(s).
3.  Press any key to proceed.
4.  Select one of the following menu items:
◦ Sanitize/Erase and Boot to Operating System
◦ Sanitize/Erase and Power System Off
◦ Sanitize/Erase and Boot to System Utilities
5.  Select one of the following menu items based on the NVDIMM-Ns to be sanitized/erased:
◦ Sanitize/Erase all NVDIMM-N in the System —Use this option for major system
reconfigurations.
◦ Sanitize/Erase all NVDIMM-N on Processor 1 —Use this option when all the NVDIMM-Ns on a
processor must be sanitized together, such as when enabling NVDIMM-N Memory Interleaving.
◦ Sanitize/Erase Processor 1 DIMM X —This option is available only when NVDIMM-N Memory
Interleaving is disabled. Use this option for adding individual NVDIMM-Ns, or for using an
NVDIMM-N again after errors occur.
◦ Sanitize/Erase all NVDIMM-N on Processor 2 —Use this option when all the NVDIMM-Ns on a
processor must be sanitized together, such as when enabling NVDIMM-N Memory Interleaving.
◦ Sanitize/Erase Processor 2 DIMM X —This option is available only when NVDIMM-N Memory
Interleaving is disabled. Use this option for adding individual NVDIMM-Ns, or for using an
NVDIMM-N again after errors occur.
When enabled, all user data in the selected NVDIMM-N is erased on the next reboot. The Sanitize
NVDIMM-N scope selections are available only after a sanitize policy is selected.
6.  Press the F10 key to save the changes.
7.  Reboot the server.
The server will reboot multiple times as part of the sanitize process. POST messages will also display
to indicate the NVDIMM-Ns that are being sanitized.</diag:procedureText>
    <diag:targetsComponentType rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/component-type/Memory%20module"/>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/DELL-EEM-2026-EEM-UEFI0386/procedure/01/item/001">
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#NamedIndividual"/>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting#ProcedureItem"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Source action_text character span [0, 254); UNNUMBERED_SOURCE_BLOCK; order is printed/text order, not an execution dependency.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Purpose assessment: NO_DOCUMENTED_CAUSE. No documented cause in this source solution group; do not invent a cause.</rdfs:comment>
    <diag:itemText rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#string">To view more information about the issue, go to the System Setup page on the BIOS HII, and then click the S150 Configuration Utility link. For more information, see the Dell PowerEdge RAID Controller (PERC) S150 Users Guide available on the support site.</diag:itemText>
    <rdfs:label xml:lang="en">Dell UEFI0386 printed item 1</rdfs:label>
    <diag:itemOrder rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#positiveInteger">1</diag:itemOrder>
    <diag:nestingLevel rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#nonNegativeInteger">0</diag:nestingLevel>
  </rdf:Description>
  <rdf:Description rdf:about="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x0029-0x0003/procedure/01/item/001">
    <diag:itemOrder rdf:datatype="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#positiveInteger">1</diag:itemOrder>
