  <rdf:Description rdf:nodeID="N146853e39b0949689dd16ed82e9a523b">
    <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0490/procedure/01/item/012"/>
    <rdfs:seeAlso rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0490"/>
    <owl:annotatedTarget rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/component-type/Cable%20or%20connector"/>
    <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting#targetsComponentType"/>
    <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="https://example.org/server-troubleshooting/data/event/HPE-IML-2026-IML-Class-0x000A-0x0490/procedure/01"/>
    <rdfs:comment xml:lang="en">Automatic source-text assessment: explicit diagnostic/corrective verb and component mention in the instruction. This is a procedure target, not necessarily the component responsible for the event. Evidence: Click the Temperatures tab and verify the temperature readings for each location on the Temperatures tab. If a hot spot is located, then check the airflow path for blockage by cables and other material. A hot spot is not an absolute temperature but is relative to a component specification. Hot spot is defined as temperature on a sensor within 3°C of a Caution threshold listed on the Temperature tab.</rdfs:comment>
    <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2002/07/owl#Axiom"/>
  </rdf:Description>
